var tafsirData_72 = {
  "1": "The surah opens with a divine command to the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him: “Say.” He is to convey this extraordinary event to the people. The command immediately establishes that what follows is not a human story but revelation from Allah. The phrase “It has been revealed to me” underscores the direct source of this knowledge.\n\nThe Prophet could not have known on his own that a group of jinn had listened to the Quran; jinn are unseen beings, hidden from human perception in their natural state. The event occurred, according to authentic reports, when the Prophet was returning from the painful mission to Ta’if. He stopped at a place called Nakhlah and recited the Quran during the night or dawn prayer. A group of jinn from Nasibin, sometimes identified as seven or nine, passed by and were captivated by the recitation.\n\nThey stopped to listen, and their hearts were transformed. They then returned to their people as warners, sharing what they had heard. The jinn themselves describe the Quran as “a wondrous recitation.” The Arabic word “‘ajaban” means something amazingly astonishing, far beyond ordinary speech.\n\nIt captivates the listener with its eloquence, its truth, its rhythm, and its profound meaning. The jinn, who possess sharp faculties and have existed for centuries, could immediately sense that this was no human poetry or magic. They recognized its divine origin. This opening is a powerful rebuke to the Quraysh.\n\nWhile the unseen creatures believed at the first hearing, the Quraysh, who shared the Prophet’s language and homeland, rejected it. The surah Al-Jinn thus contrasts the response of two different creations. The previous surah, Nuh, told of a people who rejected for 950 years; here, a group of jinn accepted in a single night. The lesson is clear: belief is not about time but about the state of the heart.\n\nThe phrase “a group of jinn” reminds us that not all jinn are devils; many are upright beings with free will, accountable to Allah. They listened, they understood, and they acted.",
  "2": "The jinn immediately declare their verdict on the Quran: “It leads to Right Guidance.” The word “yusaddiqu” in this context means it guides to the truth, to the straight path, to everything that is morally and spiritually correct. The Quran is not just information; it is a transformative guide. It leads to right guidance in belief, in worship, in character, and in dealings.\n\nThe jinn’s statement is a testimony of faith from another realm of creation. Then they affirm the consequence of that guidance: “so we believed in it.” Belief is not passive; it is the active, wholehearted acceptance of the truth. This immediate belief of the jinn shames those who hear the Quran day after day yet harden their hearts.\n\nFollowing their belief, they make a firm resolution: “and we will never associate anyone with our Lord in worship.” This is the core of tawheed. The jinn, who previously may have been involved in shirk, as later verses will explain, now utterly renounce it. They declare that they will never commit shirk—not now, not ever.\n\nThe use of “our Lord” (rabbina) is tender and personal. They have claimed Allah as their own Lord, entering into a relationship of devotion and submission. The phrase “never associate anyone” is an absolute negation. Shirk is the unforgivable sin if one dies upon it, and the jinn recognize its gravity.\n\nThis verse also refutes the polytheists of Mecca who associated idols, angels, and jinn themselves with Allah. The jinn themselves are now declaring pure monotheism. It dismantles the Qurayshi claim that the jinn are Allah’s partners or that they deserve worship. The verse teaches that the moment genuine guidance is heard and understood, it demands an immediate response: belief and the total rejection of all false gods.",
  "3": "The jinn continue their confession of purified faith by specifically refuting false claims about Allah. “Our Lord—Exalted is His Majesty” translates “jadd rabbina.” The Arabic word “jadd” can mean majesty, glory, or greatness. Some early scholars also understood it as “riches” or “self-sufficiency,” meaning that Allah is far too exalted, rich, and perfect to need a mate or child.\n\nThe jinn magnify Allah, declaring His absolute transcendence. Then they deny two core falsehoods: that He has “taken a mate” (sahibah, meaning spouse or female companion) and that He has “offspring” (walad, meaning child). Among the pagan Arabs, some claimed that the angels were Allah’s daughters. Certain jinn and deviant humans spread the idea that Allah had a spouse from among the jinn, producing offspring.\n\nThe jinn who believed now categorically reject these blasphemies. They affirm that Allah is above such needs; He is the Creator who neither begets nor is begotten. This is identical to the message of Surah Al-Ikhlas. The mention of “mate” and “offspring” reflects the depth of the corruption that had infiltrated the unseen realm.\n\nIblis and his followers had propagated these lies, as mentioned in the next verse. The jinn are now publicly repenting and setting the record straight. This verse teaches that proper exaltation of Allah requires purifying one’s concept of Him from all anthropomorphic or mythological ideas. The phrase “Exalted is His Majesty” is a form of glorification that every believer should internalize.\n\nIt echoes the teaching of Noah in the previous surah, where he asked, “What is the matter with you that you are not in awe of the Majesty of Allah?” The jinn have now answered that call; they are in awe and they proclaim that awe by denying any deficiency in the Divine.",
  "4": "The jinn now openly admit the errors of their own kind. “The foolish of us” refers to the ignorant, reckless, and unwise among the jinn—specifically Iblis and his followers, the devils who spread lies. The word “safih” denotes a person who lacks sound judgment, who is driven by whims and arrogance rather than truth. These foolish ones “used to utter outrageous falsehoods about Allah.\n\n” The Arabic “shatatan” means statements that are extreme, far from the truth, and utterly unreasonable. These falsehoods include the very claims denied in the previous verse: that Allah has a wife, that He has children, that there are beings who share His power or deserve worship alongside Him. Some commentators mention that Iblis even proclaimed that Allah had taken a spouse from the jinn and produced offspring. The believing jinn are now disassociating themselves from these blasphemies.\n\nTheir confession is a model of humility. They do not deny their past misguidance; they acknowledge that the foolish among them fell into grave error. This teaches humans that acknowledging the mistakes of one’s forefathers or community is not a betrayal but a necessary step toward truth. The Quraysh were asked to abandon the idols their ancestors worshipped, and they refused out of pride.\n\nThe jinn, however, willingly call their own past “foolishness.” The verse also warns that among any creation, there will be those who go to extremes and lead others astray. Believers must always measure claims about Allah against the clear revelation, not against tradition or speculation. By exposing the source of the lies, the jinn dismantle the foundation of idolatry.\n\nIf the very beings whom humans worshipped as intermediaries now admit that their own foolish ones lied about Allah, what justification remains for shirk?",
  "5": "This verse reveals a mistaken assumption the jinn had held for ages: “We certainly thought that humans and jinn would never speak lies about Allah.” Before hearing the Quran, the jinn assumed that no rational creature would dare fabricate falsehoods about the Creator. They had a certain naive trust that beings with intellect would never be so audacious. However, the emergence of shirk and the outrageous claims about Allah—a spouse, offspring, partners—proved them wrong.\n\nBoth humans and jinn had indeed told enormous lies about the Divine. This realization shattered their previous worldview and opened their eyes to the depth of deception that Satan had spread. The verse carries an important lesson: assumptions based on what one thinks others would or would not do are not reliable guides to truth. The only reliable guide is revelation.\n\nThe jinn’s admission also highlights that evil is real and widespread. Many people today assume that all religions are fundamentally sincere or that no one would deliberately invent false doctrines about God. History shows otherwise. The jinn’s awakening is a call for critical reflection; believers must not blindly trust inherited beliefs but must test them against the clear message of the Quran and the prophets.\n\nThe phrase “humans and jinn” also shows the unity of the two species in responsibility. Both are subject to the same spiritual dangers. They can both be misled by the foolish among them. This verse connects to the previous surahs’ themes: the disbelievers in Al-Ma‘arij thought the Day of Judgment was impossible because they had not been taught correctly.\n\nThe jinn now understand that widespread belief does not equal truth. Lies can become so common that they are mistaken for reality. The antidote is the “wondrous recitation” that exposes the falsehood.",
  "6": "The jinn now recount a specific pre-Islamic practice that deepened misguidance: “some men used to seek refuge with some jinn.” In pre-Islamic Arabia, when a person travelled and stopped in a desolate valley at night, he would call out, “I seek refuge in the master of this valley from the foolish among his people.” He was essentially asking the chief jinn of that area for protection against any harm from lesser jinn. This act of seeking refuge in other than Allah was a form of shirk.\n\nIt was based on the belief that the jinn had power independent of Allah to protect or harm. The result was devastating: “so they increased each other in wickedness.” The humans, by seeking refuge with jinn, committed shirk and lost their reliance on Allah. The jinn, being invoked and worshipped, became arrogant and more emboldened in their evil.\n\nThe humans’ fear of the jinn grew, and the jinn’s tyranny over the humans expanded. It was a vicious cycle of mutual reinforcement of sin. This verse is a timeless warning against turning to any created being—whether jinn, saint, astrologer, or amulet—for protection that should only be sought from Allah. The Prophet, peace be upon him, taught the believers to recite specific supplications for protection, such as Ayat al-Kursi and the Mu‘awwidhatayn, which direct one’s heart to Allah alone.\n\nThe jinn, in recounting this, are admitting how both species were drawn into deeper misguidance. They now recognize the evil of that practice. The verse also explains why the jinn might have felt superior; humans were grovelling to them. But Islam restored the balance by commanding humans to worship only Allah and teaching that all creation, including jinn, are His servants.\n\nThe increase in wickedness was a direct result of bypassing the Creator and clinging to creation. This lesson remains urgently relevant: reliance on anything other than Allah—be it money, status, or occult practices—only compounds one’s problems.",
  "7": "The jinn now address their fellow jinn, pointing out a shared false belief: “those humans thought, just like you jinn, that Allah would not resurrect anyone for judgment.” Many humans, especially the pagan Arabs, denied the resurrection and the Day of Reckoning. The jinn, as a community, had also fallen into this delusion, influenced by the devils among them. Both groups assumed that death was the end and that there would be no accountability.\n\nThis denial of the Hereafter was the root of much of their moral corruption. If there is no judgment, then one can live for pleasure, power, and self-gratification without fear of ultimate consequence. The jinn now realize how wrong they were. The Quran’s powerful arguments for the resurrection, its descriptions of Paradise and Hell, and its logical reasoning from the creation of the heavens and the earth awakened them to the truth.\n\nThis verse demonstrates that denial of the afterlife is a universal error that crosses species, not just a human folly. The jinn’s confession affirms that the message of all prophets, including the Quran, is essential to correct this misconception. The phrase “just like you” indicates that the jinn speaking are now distancing themselves from that past ignorance. They are calling their fellow jinn to abandon the old belief.\n\nThis connects directly to Al-Ma‘arij, where the disbelievers “truly see this Day as impossible” and to Noah’s preaching of the resurrection. The jinn who heard the Quran now understand that the One who created them in stages can indeed bring them back. The verse is a powerful reminder that the Hereafter is not a negotiable part of faith; it is a central pillar, and denying it leads to utter loss.",
  "8": "The jinn now shift to a cosmic sign that deeply affected them and signaled the arrival of a new prophetic era: “Earlier we tried to reach heaven for news.” Before the advent of the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, the jinn used to ascend toward the lowest heaven to eavesdrop on the conversations of the angels. They would snatch snippets of divine decrees—future events, fortunes, misfortunes—and pass them on to fortune-tellers and soothsayers on earth, who would then mix them with a hundred lies and gain fame and influence. But after the Prophet’s mission began, they attempted this again “only to find it filled with stern guards and shooting stars.\n\n” The “stern guards” are powerful angels stationed to protect the heavens. The “shooting stars” (shuhub) are flaming meteors hurled at any jinn attempting to steal a hearing. This was a dramatic change in the cosmic order. The sky, which was once accessible for their eavesdropping, was now a fortified barrier.\n\nThis sign, mentioned also in Surah Al-Hijr and As-Saffat, indicated that the era of soothsaying had come to an end. The truth was now descending through a protected channel: the angel Jibril bringing revelation to the Prophet. No unauthorized leaks could contaminate the pure message. The jinn recognized this event as a major turning point.\n\nIt caused them to search for the reason, leading some of them to the Quran. The verse highlights the miracle of the Quran’s preservation and the end of the chaos of false oracles. For the believers, it is a reassurance that the divine message they receive is pure and uncontaminated by demonic interference. The universe itself was rearranged to honour and protect the final revelation.",
  "9": "The jinn provide further detail about their old practice: “We used to take up positions there for eavesdropping.” They would sit in specific stations, one above another, forming a chain to catch the sound and relay it down. It was a calculated effort, a sort of cosmic espionage. But now, “whoever dares eavesdrop now will find a flare lying in wait for him.\n\n” The Arabic word “shihab” is a bright, blazing flame—a meteor that is specifically aimed at the intruder. The phrase “lying in wait” (rasadan) suggests that the angels are placed precisely to monitor and intercept any such attempt. The jinn are met with a vigilant and relentless defense. Any jinn who tries is immediately targeted and burned.\n\nThis destroyed their ability to gather secret knowledge. The jinn who heard the Quran now explain this to their fellows, acknowledging the futility of even trying. The verse underscores the absolute sovereignty of Allah; He guards His unseen knowledge. It also nullifies the credibility of fortune-tellers and magicians who previously relied on stolen information.\n\nTheir source is now permanently cut off. For the believers, this is a call to trust only in the revealed Quran and to shun all forms of divination. The jinn’s candid admission is a powerful testimony that something monumental had occurred in the heavens, and the only sensible response was to seek out the new messenger and submit. The “flare lying in wait” is a physical manifestation of the divine wrath against those who attempt to trespass on the realm of the unseen.\n\nIt shows that the unseen is not a realm of chaos but is meticulously governed by Allah’s command.",
  "10": "With the eavesdropping blocked, the jinn are plunged into a state of ignorance about the future: “Now, we have no clue whether evil is intended for those on earth, or their Lord intends for them what is right.” Before, they could snatch fragments of information and speculate about impending disasters or blessings. Now, they are completely cut off. They candidly confess their uncertainty.\n\nThis admission is profoundly important. It demolishes the illusion that jinn have innate knowledge of the unseen or can predict the future. They are as ignorant as humans when it comes to Allah’s plans. The phrase “evil is intended” could refer to a destructive punishment, perhaps a flood or earthquake.\n\n“What is right” refers to mercy, guidance, and prosperity. The jinn heard the strong warning in the Quran and saw the heavens fortified; they wondered if a catastrophe was about to strike the earth. But they could not be sure; only Allah knows. This uncertainty drove them to embrace the clear revelation they had heard, because the Quran provides the answers that stolen whispers could never give.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer not to seek knowledge of the future from any creature. The unseen is Allah’s exclusive domain. The jinn’s humility in admitting “we have no clue” is a model for all intelligent beings. It contrasts with human soothsayers who pretend to know what will happen.\n\nThe verse also connects to the earlier surahs where the punishment was a central theme; the jinn are now aware that a warning has come, and they choose to believe rather than risk facing an unknown calamity unprepared. Their statement reflects a healthy fear of the Lord’s plan, which leads them to take the safest path: embracing the guidance.",
  "11": "The jinn now describe the diversity within their own species, a fact often overlooked by humans who stereotype all jinn as evil. “Among us are those who are righteous.” The word “salihun” means good, upright, obedient to Allah. These are the believing jinn who accept the truth and live by it.\n\n“And those who are less so” translates a phrase indicating those who are not righteous—some are mediocre, some are outright evil. This spectrum of morality mirrors the human condition. The jinn then say, “We have been of different factions.” The word “shiya‘” means diverse groups, sects, and paths.\n\nBefore Islam, the jinn were divided into various religions and ideologies, just like humans. Some were Jews, Christians, Magians, or idolaters, influenced by the cultures near which they resided. Now, the Quran has come to unite the righteous among them on the single path of Islam. The verse serves multiple purposes.\n\nIt corrects the false notion that all jinn are demons; they have free will and are accountable. It also warns humans that the jinn have their own struggles with faith and divisions, so no one should invoke them as gods or intermediaries, because they themselves are in need of guidance. The fact that the righteous jinn openly acknowledge their community’s diversity shows their sincerity. They are not hiding their internal problems.\n\nFor believers, the lesson is to avoid generalizations and to understand that the struggle between good and evil exists in all intelligent species. The jinn’s testimony makes their embrace of Islam even more compelling; they chose unity over factionalism, truth over inherited division.",
  "12": "Having witnessed the cosmic events and heard the Quran, the believing jinn have attained a deep conviction: “we truly know that we cannot frustrate Allah on earth, nor can we escape from Him into heaven.” The word “lan nu‘jiza Allah” means we can never render Him incapable, we can never outrun His power or thwart His decree. Whether on earth or attempting to flee to the heavens, there is absolutely no escape. This knowledge is the fruit of belief.\n\nBefore, the jinn roamed the skies and thought they had access to hidden realms. Now they realize that even the heavens are under strict divine control, and their previous eavesdropping was only by permission. The phrase “nor can we escape from Him into heaven” is a direct reference to their failed attempts to reach the heavenly assembly. If they try to flee upward, they are met with shooting stars.\n\nIf they stay on earth, they are still within His grip. There is no corner of the universe that lies beyond Allah’s authority. This admission is a declaration of total submission. It echoes the message of Al-Ma‘arij, where the angels ascend through the pathways to a Lord whose punishment cannot be averted.\n\nIt also parallels Noah’s people who found no help against Allah. The jinn have learned the lesson that many humans ignore: no one can outmaneuver the Almighty. This knowledge produces a healthy fear and a commitment to obedience. For the believer, reflecting on this verse instills a sense of awe and accountability.\n\nWhether one is in the depths of the sea or the heights of the sky, Allah’s knowledge and power encompass everything. True freedom lies not in running but in surrendering to the only inescapable reality.",
  "13": "The jinn return to the pivotal moment of their transformation: “When we heard the guidance of the Quran, we readily believed in it.” The word “guidance” (huda) captures the essence of the Quran; it is not merely a book of laws but a guiding light. Their belief was immediate and without hesitation, indicated by the emphatic “readily.” They did not demand miracles, they did not argue, they did not stall.\n\nThis contrasts starkly with the Quraysh, who were offered the same guidance but engaged in endless disputes. The jinn then explain the benefit of such belief: “For whoever believes in their Lord will have no fear of being denied a reward or wronged.” The phrase “no fear of being denied a reward” means they will not suffer any decrease in the good deeds they earned. Allah will not shortchange them; every good act, however small, will be accounted and rewarded fully.\n\n“Or wronged” means they will not be unjustly punished for sins they did not commit, nor will their bad deeds be exaggerated. The believer is secure in Allah’s perfect justice. This assurance is a powerful motivator. The jinn, who previously lived in a world of uncertainty and fear of the unknown, now find security in their Lord’s promise.\n\nThe verse highlights that faith in Allah brings ultimate fairness. Human systems of justice are flawed; people often lose rights or face oppression. But with Allah, no injustice exists. The believer can rest assured that his efforts are not wasted.\n\nThis message would have been a balm for the persecuted early Muslims in Mecca, who were denied their rights and wronged daily. The jinn’s testimony affirms that the ultimate court is with Allah, and there, no injustice will be tolerated.",
  "14": "The jinn restate the division within their community, now with clearer terminology: “among us are those who have submitted to Allah and those who are deviant.” Submission (islam) is the voluntary surrender of one’s will to Allah, which is the very definition of the religion brought by all prophets. The “deviant” (qaasitun) are those who turn away from the straight path, who incline toward injustice and falsehood. Then a definitive verdict is given: “So as for those who submitted, it is they who have attained Right Guidance.\n\n” This is an unambiguous statement that true guidance is only found in Islam, in the submission to the One God. The deviants are excluded from guidance by their own choice. The jinn are acting as preachers to their own kind, making the truth clear. They do not promote pluralism; they declare that right guidance is exclusive to those who submit.\n\nThis is not arrogance but a sincere warning born from their own experience of finding the truth. The verse also implicitly addresses the human listeners. If even the jinn can discern that submission is the only way to guidance, how can the pagans of Mecca remain in their deviance? The phrase “it is they who have attained Right Guidance” uses the Arabic “fahuwa” to emphasize that these are indeed the rightly guided.\n\nThe word “rashad” means maturity, sound judgment, and hitting the target. The submitter has reached spiritual maturity. The verse connects to the core message of Al-Ma‘arij and Noah: success lies in faith and good deeds, not in wealth, clan, or power. The jinn who submitted are now among the blessed, while the deviant among them face the same fate as deviant humans.",
  "15": "The jinn conclude their speech with a stark warning to their own species: “And as for the deviant, they will be fuel for Hell.” The word “deviant” (qaasitun) describes those who stubbornly turn away from the truth. Their ultimate destination is Hellfire, where they will serve as its “fuel” (hatab). This is the same imagery used in the Quran for human disbelievers.\n\nThe jinn are not a different class of creation with a separate destiny; they will be thrown into the same Fire along with sinful humans. This verse clearly establishes that jinn are accountable and will be judged. The righteous among them enter Paradise, and the deviant enter Hell. This demolishes any notion that jinn are gods or that they have a special status exempting them from divine justice.\n\nTheir mention as “fuel” indicates they will burn and suffer the torment, contributing to the intensity of the Fire. The lesson for humans is that if the jinn, despite their powers and their ability to roam the heavens, cannot escape Hell if they deviate, then humans should certainly not feel secure. The jinn’s own testimony becomes a powerful tool of da‘wah to both species. For the believer, this verse deepens the fear of deviation and the gratitude for the guidance that leads to safety.\n\nIt also reassures that the oppression and mockery faced from the deviant, whether human or jinn, will not go unpunished. The justice of Allah is all-encompassing. The speech of the jinn ends here, and the surah pivots back to addressing the human deniers of Mecca, drawing a direct lesson from what the jinn said.",
  "16": "The focus now shifts entirely to the human deniers, the Quraysh and their ilk. Allah declares a conditional statement: “Had the deniers followed the Right Way, We would have certainly granted them abundant rain to drink.” The “Right Way” (tariqah) refers to the path of Islam, the straight religion of monotheism and righteousness. The promise attached to it is abundant rain.\n\nIn the arid climate of Arabia, rain was synonymous with life, prosperity, and survival. The connection between spiritual uprightness and material blessings is a recurring theme. Just as Noah promised his people that seeking forgiveness would bring rain and wealth, here Allah affirms the same principle. The word “ma’an ghadaqan” means plentiful, gushing water.\n\nIt signifies not just a single shower but a continuous, generous supply. The verse teaches that the struggles of the Quraysh, including the drought they sometimes faced, were linked to their rejection of the truth. If they would only accept tawheed, the heavens would open. This is not a superstitious belief but a divine law: when societies obey Allah, He blesses them with the resources of the earth.\n\nWhen they rebel, those blessings are withheld or become a trial. The verse serves as both an invitation and a warning. The invitation is clear: embrace the truth and experience material ease as a sign of Allah’s pleasure. The warning is implicit: if you continue in denial, do not expect sustained prosperity.\n\nThis verse also connects to the jinn’s earlier words about not knowing whether evil or good was intended for earth. Here, Allah clarifies that the good would have come had they chosen the right path. The deniers are given a glimpse of what they lost.",
  "17": "The verse continues with a crucial qualification: “as a test for them.” Even the abundant rain and worldly blessings would not be an unconditional reward but a test. Would the people be grateful and increase in faith, or would they become arrogant and attribute the prosperity to their own efforts or to their idols? Wealth and ease are heavy trials.\n\nMany are ruined by abundance more than by poverty. Allah would test the deniers with the very blessings they desired to see if they would live up to their responsibility. Then the verse delivers a stern warning: “And whoever turns away from the remembrance of their Lord will be admitted by Him into an overwhelming punishment.” The “remembrance of their Lord” refers to the Quran, the message of truth.\n\nTurning away from it after hearing it is an act of rebellion. The consequence is an “overwhelming punishment” (adhab sa‘d). The word “sa‘d” describes a punishment that is severe, ascending in intensity, or climbing over the one who suffers it. It is a torment that crushes all resistance and leaves no escape.\n\nSome commentators say it implies a punishment that is uphill, a painful climb into increasing distress. This connects to Al-Ma‘arij, where the punishment was a raging Flame that rips scalps. The verse establishes that worldly blessings, had they come, would not have been a sign of final approval but a test before the ultimate judgment. And for those who reject the message, no amount of rain in this world will save them from the drought of mercy in the Hereafter.\n\nThe turning away is a conscious choice, and the punishment is the inevitable result of that choice. The verse calls the listener to think beyond immediate comfort and consider the eternal outcome.",
  "18": "This verse lays down a fundamental principle of worship: “The places of worship are only for Allah.” The word “masajid” is plural of masjid, the place of prostration. It refers primarily to the physical mosques where prayers are offered, but its meaning extends to any place on earth where one prostrates to Allah, as the Prophet, peace be upon him, said, “The whole earth has been made a masjid for me.” The mosques are to be dedicated exclusively to the worship of the One God.\n\nNo idols, no statues, no shrines for saints, no invocations to any being other than Allah should be found within them. The command “so do not invoke anyone besides Him” applies within the mosques and beyond them. The act of invocation (du‘a) is worship itself, as the Prophet taught, “Du‘a is worship.” Calling upon someone else, whether a prophet, an angel, a jinn, or an idol, is an act of shirk that nullifies the purpose of the mosque.\n\nThis verse was revealed in Mecca, where the Kaaba, built by Abraham as a pure house of worship, had been filled with 360 idols. The Quraysh had turned the sacred mosque into a pantheon. The verse is a direct assault on that practice, declaring that these places belong solely to Allah. It also connects to the jinn’s testimony of pure monotheism.\n\nThe jinn affirmed they would never associate anyone with their Lord, and now the human deniers are commanded to ensure that even the architecture of their worship reflects that same purity. The practical lesson for all believers is to guard the masjids from innovations and shirk, and to fill them with the remembrance of Allah alone. The heart is also a masjid where Allah should be worshipped exclusively, free from the idols of desires and worldly attachments.",
  "19": "This verse describes a stark contrast to the command just given. While the mosques are for Allah alone, when the greatest servant of Allah, the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, “stood up calling upon Him alone” in prayer and recitation, “the pagans almost swarmed over him.” The Arabic “yakaduna yakununa ‘alayhi libad” paints a vivid image: they crowded around him so densely, they were almost a compact mass, pressing in on him. This occurred when the Prophet prayed openly near the Kaaba or in other locations, reciting the Quran and calling upon Allah.\n\nThe disbelievers would gather from all sides, as mentioned in Al-Ma‘arij where they rushed “from the right and the left, in groups to mock you.” Here, their physical aggression nearly reached the point of crushing him. The phrase “servant of Allah” (‘abd Allah) is the most honourable title any human can have. It signifies complete submission and devotion.\n\nThe Prophet is called the servant of Allah in the context of prayer, highlighting his perfect worship. The Quraysh could not tolerate seeing someone pray to Allah alone, because it exposed the falsehood of their idols and their traditions. Their reaction was not intellectual debate but animalistic mob fury. This verse reassures the Prophet that his pure monotheism is what provokes them, but he is on the right path.\n\nIt also contrasts the Quraysh’s behaviour with that of the jinn. The jinn listened quietly, believed, and warned their people. The Quraysh listened only to attack. The verse is a timeless lesson: when a believer stands firm upon tawheed, the forces of falsehood may swarm, but the believer must stand his ground, for the mosques belong to Allah, not to the mob.",
  "20": "In the face of the swarming pagans, Allah commands the Prophet to make a clear, unwavering declaration: “Say, O Prophet, ‘I call only upon my Lord, associating none with Him in worship.’” This is a statement of absolute monotheism and a direct answer to the polytheists who wanted him to compromise. The verb “call” (ad‘u) encompasses all forms of worship and supplication. The Prophet declares that his worship, his prayers, his requests, his reliance—all of it is directed solely to his Lord. He then explicitly negates any shirk: “associating none with Him.\n\n” The phrase “in worship” clarifies that this association pertains to any act of devotion. This public testimony places a clear boundary between him and the idolaters. There is no middle ground; he will not call upon their idols alongside Allah, nor will he invoke angels, jinn, or saints. The repetition of the first-person possessive “my Lord” is intimate and exclusive.\n\nIt is as if he is saying: my Lord is the only One I call; your lords and idols have no share. This verse is the Quranic response to all pressures to syncretize faith or dilute monotheism. It echoes the jinn’s earlier declaration: “we will never associate anyone with our Lord.” The same words are now placed on the Prophet’s tongue.\n\nThe lesson is that the essence of Islam is pure tawheed, and it must be proclaimed courageously, even when the opposition is ready to swarm. For the believer, this verse is a model of steadfastness. In a world that often demands religious compromise, the response must remain: I call only upon my Lord, no one else. The surah’s argument now builds toward reminding the Prophet of his limited role and the ultimate accountability before Allah.",
  "21": "After the Prophet is commanded to declare his exclusive worship of Allah, he is now instructed to clarify the limits of his own human capacity. The disbelievers of Quraysh often challenged him, demanding that he bring down the punishment they were warned about, or that he perform miracles on command, or that he grant them some worldly benefit to prove his truthfulness. They treated him almost as if he claimed divine powers. This verse resets the understanding: “Say, ‘It is not in my power to harm or benefit you.’” The Arabic “la amliku” means “I do not own, I do not possess, I have no control over” any harm or benefit for you.\n\nThe Prophet, peace be upon him, despite being the best of creation, is still a servant. He cannot cause a person to become sick or healthy, poor or rich, guided or misguided. All power belongs to Allah alone. This is a profound statement of tawheed in action.\n\nIt prevents any exaggeration of the Prophet’s status, closing the door to the kind of saint-worship that corrupted earlier nations. He cannot independently harm even those who mock him, nor can he grant paradise or worldly success to his most beloved followers. His role is purely to convey. This verse also answers the psychological state of the pagans who feared that the Prophet’s prayer might bring a curse upon them.\n\nThey accused him of being a sorcerer who could harm people with his words. He is to clarify that even if he wished to harm them, he has no power to do so unless Allah wills it. Similarly, he cannot force benefit upon them, such as forcing faith into their hearts. The verse emphasizes the absolute dependence of all creation on Allah.\n\nThe Prophet is a mercy to the worlds, but that mercy is a reflection of Allah’s mercy, not an independent power. For believers, this instills reliance on Allah alone. Even the Prophet cannot be a substitute for the Creator. The verse also provides comfort to the Prophet; he is not responsible for the outcome, only for the delivery.\n\nHe does not need to feel burdened by their rejection as if he had failed to compel them.",
  "22": "This verse deepens the theme of the Prophet’s servanthood and humanity. After declaring his lack of power to harm or benefit others, he is told to announce his own vulnerability before Allah. “No one can protect me from Allah if I were to disobey Him.” The Arabic “innee lan yujeeranee min Allahi ahad” is a powerful negation: never, under any circumstance, could anyone grant him protection from Allah’s punishment if he were to disobey.\n\nThis includes all the jinn who might have been thought to have power, all the angels, all the idols, and all the Quraysh chiefs. Even the Prophet, the most beloved of creation to Allah, is not given a blank cheque of immunity. If he were to deviate from the revelation, no intercessor could shield him. This statement demolishes any notion of prophetic infallibility meaning exemption from accountability; rather, the prophets are protected from persisting in major sin, but they live with the constant awe of Allah’s justice.\n\nSome Quraysh had suggested that the Prophet should compromise, maybe worship their gods for a year and they would worship his God. This verse is a rebuke: even if he desired to please them, he could not, for the fear of Allah’s punishment is his shield. Then the verse adds, “nor can I find any refuge other than Him.” The word “multahadan” means a place of retreat, a shelter, a sanctuary.\n\nNo mountain, no cave, no powerful ally can offer him a hiding place from Allah’s decree. This echoes the jinn’s earlier admission that they cannot escape Allah on earth or in heaven. The Prophet is aligning himself fully with the position of a dependent servant. The lesson for the believers is that if the Prophet himself lives with this consciousness, how much more should ordinary Muslims fear disobedience?\n\nNo amount of piety or good deeds allows one to feel secure from Allah’s plan. The only true refuge is in Allah Himself, through His mercy. The verse, therefore, is a powerful call to humility, stripping away any reliance on human status or connections.",
  "23": "Having clarified what he cannot do, the Prophet now states positively what his mission is: “My duty is only to convey the truth from Allah and deliver His messages.” The word “illa” (only) restricts his role. He is a messenger, a carrier of the divine message. The phrase “the truth from Allah” refers to the Quran and the guidance that leads to righteousness.\n\n“His messages” (risalatihi) encompasses all the teachings, laws, and warnings that form the complete religion. Conveying (balagh) means to deliver clearly, completely, and without distortion. This is the sole obligation for which he will be held accountable. The verse thus divides responsibility: the Prophet conveys, and the people choose.\n\nNo one can blame him if they reject, because he has fulfilled the duty. This relieves the Prophet of the psychological weight of their rejection; their faith is not in his hands. The verse then abruptly shifts to a stern warning: “And whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger will certainly be in the Fire of Hell, to stay there for ever and ever.” Disobedience here refers to kufr and rebellion against the message conveyed.\n\nThe punishment is eternal Hellfire. This warning is directed squarely at the pagan deniers who hear the Prophet’s message but refuse to obey. The phrase “to stay there for ever and ever” (khalideena fiha abada) emphasizes the permanence for those who die in disbelief. This is consistent with the Quranic teaching that Hell is eternal for the disbelievers.\n\nThe contrast is intentional: the Prophet only conveys, but those who reject the conveyance face eternal ruin. It also serves as a reminder to the believers that obedience to the Messenger is not optional; it is obedience to Allah. The verse beautifully encapsulates the balance of Islamic da‘wah: a clear, non-coercive presentation of truth, coupled with a serious warning of the consequence of rejection. The Prophet’s mission is mercy, but it carries the weight of the ultimate reality.",
  "24": "The surah now turns to the disbelievers who mocked and challenged the Prophet, boasting of their superior numbers and strength. “Only when they see what they have been threatened with will they know...” The “what they have been threatened with” is the Day of Judgment, the punishment of the Fire, the very reality they denied and demanded to see immediately. The phrase “only when they see” implies that their current ignorance and arrogance will last until that moment of shock.\n\nThen, and only then, will they “know who is weaker in helpers and inferior in manpower.” In this world, the Quraysh considered the believers a weak minority. They thought their own numerous warriors, their tribal alliances, and their wealth made them powerful. They mocked the small band of Muslims, saying they had no helpers and no military strength.\n\nBut on the Day of Judgment, the roles will be reversed. The disbelievers will find themselves utterly alone, stripped of their armies, their wealth, their clan support. No helper will come to their aid, as was repeatedly stated in Al-Ma‘arij. The believers, on the other hand, will have the angels, the prophets, and ultimately Allah as their helper.\n\nThe disbelievers will finally realize that the Muslims, whom they considered weak, were the truly powerful ones because they were backed by the Almighty. The phrase “weaker in helpers” means they will see their own complete lack of intercessors. “Inferior in manpower” means their numbers will count for nothing. This verse functions as a delayed answer to all the taunts.\n\nIt tells the believers to be patient; the true evaluation of strength is not in this world’s scales but will be revealed on the Day of the Final Unveiling. The jinn had already acknowledged that they cannot frustrate Allah; here, the same truth is applied to the human deniers. The verse instills hope in the oppressed and terror in the arrogant oppressors.",
  "25": "The Quraysh repeatedly asked, “When will this promise be fulfilled?” They demanded to know the exact timing of the Day of Judgment, hoping to catch the Prophet in a contradiction or to dismiss it as remote. Allah commands the Prophet to respond with complete honesty: “Say, ‘I do not know if what you are promised is near or my Lord has set a distant time for it.’” This is a striking admission of the Prophet’s lack of knowledge of the unseen. Even the final messenger, the most knowledgeable of creation about revelation, does not know the Hour.\n\nThe knowledge of its timing is one of the keys of the unseen that Allah has kept exclusively to Himself. The Prophet’s humility is a refutation of any claim to divinity. He is a servant, not an oracle who can answer every question. The phrase “what you are promised” is the punishment and the Day of Reckoning.\n\nBy saying “I do not know,” the Prophet shuts down their line of interrogation and redirects their focus. The important thing is not when it will happen, but the certainty that it will happen and the need to prepare for it. The nearness or distance is relative; in the scale of eternity, it is always near. The verse also connects to the jinn’s earlier statement that they had no clue whether evil or good was intended for those on earth.\n\nBoth jinn and Prophet affirm their ignorance of the specific timing, making it clear that this knowledge belongs to Allah alone. For the believers, this teaches contentment with what is known and trust in the wisdom of what is hidden. Obsessing over the timing of the end is a distraction from the duty of the present. The verse thus beautifully balances urgency (“it could be near”) with patience (“or my Lord has set a distant time”), leaving the servant in a state of vigilant preparedness without anxiety.",
  "26": "The surah now reveals why the Prophet does not know the timing: “He is the Knower of the unseen, disclosing none of it to anyone.” Allah alone possesses the absolute, comprehensive knowledge of ghayb—all that is hidden from creation, past, present, and future. The “unseen” includes the timing of the Hour, the events of the future, the secrets of hearts, the realities of the spiritual realms. The phrase “disclosing none of it to anyone” means that in principle, Allah does not grant unrestricted access to His unseen knowledge to any creature.\n\nNo angel, no prophet, no jinn has a general database of the unseen. This is a fundamental Islamic creed. The jinn, who used to eavesdrop on heavenly news, were cut off precisely because that knowledge is Allah’s exclusive domain. The verse demolishes the claims of fortune-tellers, astrologers, and so-called saints who pretend to know the future or the unseen.\n\nEven the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, had no independent knowledge of the unseen; what he knew of it came only through specific revelation that Allah chose to give him. The verse sets the stage for the exception in the next verse, but for now it establishes the rule: the unseen is a sealed treasure with Allah. For believers, this is immensely comforting. The future is in the hands of the All-Knowing and All-Wise.\n\nThere is no need to seek glimpses of it through illicit means. It also humbles the believer, reminding us that our knowledge is minuscule. The natural curiosity to know what will happen is answered by trusting that the Knower of the unseen has everything planned with perfect wisdom. The verse also strengthens the Prophet’s position: his inability to answer the “when” question is not a weakness but a proof of his truthfulness, for he only claims what Allah reveals.",
  "27": "The previous verse established the rule: Allah does not disclose the unseen to anyone. Now the exception is given: “except messengers of His choice.” This is a crucial clarification. Allah does select certain individuals—the prophets and messengers—and reveals to them portions of the unseen as He wills, to enable them to fulfill their missions.\n\nThis revelation is not a general access; it is specific, controlled, and for a purpose. The Prophet received knowledge of the Hereafter, stories of past nations, and certain future events that were relevant to his message. However, even with this exception, the knowledge is not independently acquired; it is a gift. The verse then adds a beautiful description of how this revelation is protected: “Then He appoints angel-guards before and behind them.\n\n” The “them” refers to the messengers, or some say to the revelation itself. The angels are stationed in front and behind, forming a protective escort. This ensures that the message reaches the messenger without any interception or tampering by devils. This is a direct contrast to the jinn’s earlier description of how they used to eavesdrop but were met with shooting stars.\n\nThe divine revelation is guarded at every stage: the angel Jibril brings it down, and other angels surround the process to prevent any demonic interference. Some commentators mention that the guards protect the messenger himself, keeping away any harmful jinn or evil forces that might try to disrupt his mission. The imagery is of a precious, guarded convoy moving through the cosmos. This assures the believers that the Quran they receive is pure, authentic, and unchanged.\n\nThe chain of transmission from the Knower of the unseen to His chosen messenger is sealed and secure. It is a testament to the trustworthiness of the Prophet and the absolute reliability of the Quran. The jinn’s failed eavesdropping is the flip side of this; they could not touch the guarded revelation.",
  "28": "The final verse of the surah explains the ultimate purpose of that angelic guard: “to ensure that the messengers fully deliver the messages of their Lord.” The Arabic “li-ya‘lama” means “that He may know” in the sense of knowledge that is manifest and established as fact. The guarding angels accompany the messengers so that it becomes clear and confirmed that the messengers have indeed conveyed every part of the divine message, without addition, subtraction, or corruption. This is a profound assurance to the Prophet and to the believers: the delivery of the message is not left to chance.\n\nAllah provides the means to guarantee its preservation. The phrase “though He already knows all about them” clarifies that Allah’s knowledge is not dependent on this process; He knows, in His eternal knowledge, that they will deliver the message. But the process of sending guards, witnessing, and recording establishes the proof for creation. It demonstrates the justice of Allah; on the Day of Judgment, there will be no doubt that the messengers conveyed the truth.\n\nThe final phrase “and keeps account of everything” (ahsa kulla shay’in ‘adada) is a magnificent closure. Allah has enumerated, counted, and accounted for every single thing in existence. Nothing escapes His knowledge—not the number of angels, not the words of the message, not the deeds of creation. The word “adada” emphasizes precise counting.\n\nThis connects back to the jinn’s realization that they cannot escape Allah, and to the warning that the disbelievers will be in Hell forever. Everything is under meticulous divine record. The surah, which began with the jinn hearing a wondrous recitation, ends with the grand affirmation that this recitation is part of a perfectly guarded, fully accounted system of divine communication. It reassures the Prophet of his mission’s integrity, warns the deniers of the certainty of their reckoning, and lifts the believers’ hearts to trust in the Lord who guards His message and counts all things."
};
